              "text": "placeō, placēre — \"to please, be pleasing\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Haec domus nova uxōrī meae placet. This new house pleases my wife."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "placeō, placēre, placuī, placitum, with the dative: mihi placet — I like it; it seems good to me. English please comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "placeō is a native Latin verb, a cousin of plācō \"to calm\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English please and pleasure come from it through Old French."
            }
          ]
        },
